.TH CAGE 1 "2026-05-31" "cage" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cage \- simple container\-like process isolation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cage
[options...] [\-\-] [\fIcommand\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBcage\fR runs a command inside an isolated environment backed by OverlayFS.
It uses Linux namespaces, bind mounts, and user/group switching to provide
filesystem and process isolation without requiring a daemon.
.P
Cage is a single static binary with no runtime dependencies.
.P
The \fIcommand\fR argument accepts two special forms:
.RS
.TP
\fB\-\fR
Run the user's shell instead of a named command.
Shell resolution: \fB\-e\fR \fISHELL=path\fR \(rarr guest passwd shell \(rarr \fI/bin/sh\fR.
The shell is invoked as a login shell (\fB\-\fIbase\fR argv[0]).
.TP
\fB\-\-\fR
When \fB\-\-\fR is given with no following command, the behavior is the same
as \fB\-\fR: the user's shell is run.
When \fB\-\-\fR is given with a following command, the command is run as\-is
(standard POSIX \fB\-\-\fR behavior).
Without \fB\-\-\fR, bare \fBcage\fR with no command exits with an error.
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Application directories"
.TP
.BI \-a " <appdir>"
Application base directory.
.RS
Default: \fI/var/lib/cage/default\fR (no application name) or
\fI/var/lib/cage/app-${name}\fR (with \fB\-n\fR).
Used only to compute \fBupperdir\fR and \fBworkdir\fR.
If no custom \fB\-U\fR is given and \fBlowerdir\fR is a parent directory on
the same filesystem, cage creates a 10G ext4 image at
\fI/var/lib/cage-loop.img\fR, mounts it at
\fI/var/lib/cage-loop\fR, and uses it as the application base
instead.
.RE
.TP
.BI \-l " <dir>"
OverlayFS lower (read\-only) directory.  Default: \fI/\fP.
.TP
.BI \-U " <dir>"
OverlayFS upper (writable) directory.  Default: \fI${appdir}/upper\fP.
Must not have \fBlowerdir\fR as a parent directory on the same filesystem.
.TP
.BI \-w " <dir>"
OverlayFS work directory.  Default: \fI${appdir}/work\fP.
Must be on the same filesystem as \fBupperdir\fR.
.SS "Namespaces"
.TP
.B \-C
Unshare the cgroup namespace.
.TP
.B \-N
Unshare the network namespace.
.SS "User and group"
.TP
.BI \-u " <user>"
User name or UID to run the command as.  Default: \fB0\fR.
Colon\-separated groups may follow the user (e.g. \fB1000:1001:1002\fR).
.TP
.BI \-g " <group>"
Group name or GID.  Default: \fB0\fR.
Colon\-separated for multiple groups.
If \fB\-u\fR is given, supplementary groups are computed automatically.
.SS "Environment"
.TP
.BI \-e " <key=value>"
Add an environment variable passed into the container.
May be given multiple times.
.TP
.BI \-c " <dir>"
Working directory inside the container.  Default: \fI/\fP.
.SS "Filesystem and volumes"
.TP
.BI \-v " <host:container>"
Map a volume from the host into the container.
If a colon is present, the left side is the host path and the right side is
the mount point inside the container.
May be given multiple times.
.SS "Lifecycle"
.TP
.BI \-i " <file>"
Init script to run as root inside the container before the target command.
.TP
.B \-k
Stop/kill the running instance identified by \fB\-p\fR or \fB\-n\fR.
Sends SIGTERM followed by SIGKILL after a timeout.
.SS "Naming"
.TP
.B \-p
.IR <file>
PID file path.
.RS
Default: \fI/run/cage/pid\fR (no application name) or
\fI/run/cage/${name}.pid\fR (with \fB\-n\fR).
.RE
.SS "Re-entry"
.TP
.B \-r
Re\-enter an existing running instance.
Joins PID, cgroup, network, and mount namespaces of the
running process identified by \fB\-p\fR or \fB\-n\fR,
then forks and runs the specified command inside those namespaces.
.SS "Miscellaneous"
.TP
.B \-G
Setup environment and volumes to support GUI applications
(e.g. X11 socket, DRI device).
.TP
.B \-h
Print help and exit.
.TP
.B \-V
Print version and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
Success.
.TP
1
General error (invalid argument, setup failure).
.TP
\fIexit code of command\fR
The exit status of the target command is forwarded to the caller.
.TP
128+\fIN\fR
The target command was terminated by signal \fIN\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
.P
Run a shell with default settings:
.IP
.B cage /bin/sh
.P
Run the user's shell (login shell, resolved from \fB\-e SHELL\fP, passwd, or \fI/bin/sh\fP):
.IP
.B cage \-
.P
Run the user's shell with \fB\-\-\fP (no command):
.IP
.B cage \-\-
.P
Run as a specific user with a custom lower directory:
.IP
.B cage \-u 1000 \-l /mnt/rootfs /bin/bash
.P
Add environment variables and map a volume:
.IP
.B cage \-e PATH=/usr/bin \-v /data:/mnt/data /bin/app
.P
Kill a running instance by name:
.IP
.B cage \-k \-n myapp
.P
Run with an init script and graphical support:
.IP
.B cage \-G \-i /tmp/setup.sh /bin/gui\-program
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/cage/default
Default application base directory (when no \fB\-n\fR is given).
.TP
.I /var/lib/cage/app-${name}
Application base directory for named instances.
.TP
.I /var/lib/cage-loop.img
Ext4 image file created automatically when applicable.
.TP
.I /run/cage/pid
Default PID file (when no \fB\-n\fR is given).
.SH BUGS
Report bugs at the project repository.
.SH AUTHOR
Tiago Augusto Pimenta
.SH SEE ALSO
